2026-05-05 22:30:17.432 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls are highly effective at detecting and preventing the Dial Out technique (T0876) in most scenarios. The control directly addresses the core mechanism of the technique—establishing outbound connections from ICS devices to external destinations. Firewalls can enforce strict egress filtering rules that either deny unauthorized outbound connections entirely (allowlist approach) or block known malicious destinations (denylist approach). In a well-configured environment with proper network segmentation, firewalls can prevent ICS devices from making unexpected outbound connections by default, which would detect any dial-out attempts that deviate from baseline traffic patterns. The control's ability to implement stateful inspection and protocol-aware filtering further enhances detection by identifying suspicious connection patterns or protocols being used for command and control. However, the rating does not reach 5 because: (1) sophisticated attackers may use legitimate protocols or ports that are already permitted through the firewall for normal operations, making detection difficult; (2) if outbound HTTPS or DNS traffic is permitted for operational reasons, these channels could be abused for command and control without triggering firewall alerts; (3) the effectiveness depends heavily on proper configuration and maintenance of firewall rules, which may not account for all possible dial-out vectors; and (4) encrypted traffic makes it difficult for stateless or basic stateful firewalls to identify malicious payloads within permitted connections. These limitations mean that while firewalls will detect most dial-out attempts, determined attackers using allowed protocols or ports may evade detection."}
