2026-05-06 07:32:18.340 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls provide limited detection capability for Transitive Access attacks. While firewalls excel at access control and network segmentation—which are preventive measures that can reduce the attack surface by limiting trust relationships between networks—they offer minimal detective capabilities for this technique. Firewalls can identify unauthorized traffic crossing network boundaries if they generate alerts on policy violations, but transitive access exploitation often leverages legitimate, pre-established trust relationships that firewalls are intentionally configured to permit. An attacker using transitive access would typically route traffic through trusted pathways (e.g., supplier networks, partner systems, or management interfaces) that firewalls allow by design. Detection would only occur if: (1) the attacker deviates significantly from normal traffic patterns in ways that violate firewall rules, (2) the firewall generates suspicious activity alerts (limited for most stateful/protocol-aware firewalls without additional IDS functionality), or (3) the attacker makes implementation errors. Skilled adversaries would route exploitation traffic through pre-authorized trust relationships, rendering firewall-based detection ineffective. Therefore, while firewalls play an important preventive role in limiting transitive access opportunities through segmentation and access control, their detection capability against this technique is weak, covering only unskilled attackers or those making significant operational mistakes."}
