2026-05-31 15:00:48.728 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0864, technique_name=Transient Cyber Asset, technique_description=Adversaries may target devices that are transient across ICS networks and external networks. Normally, transient assets are brought into an environment by authorized personnel and do not remain in that environment on a permanent basis. Transient assets are commonly needed to support management functions and may be more common in systems where a remotely managed asset is not feasible, external connections for remote access do not exist, or 3rd party contractor/vendor access is required., control_name=Peripheral device control, control_description=Peripheral device control is the governance and restriction of external hardware that can attach to an endpoint, such as keyboards, removable storage or wireless adapters.   Such channels can be used by attackers to introduce malware, exfiltrate data, or establish connectivity into the control environment. Because removable media remains one of the most reliable ways to cross an air gap, this control directly counters a primary infection vector for ICS-targeting malware and limits both inbound (malware delivery) and outbound (data theft, unauthorised remote access) misuse of physical interfaces.

On general purpose computing devices, the ability to add peripheral devices can frequently be restricted via OS-level settings or EDR and EDM tools, either entirely removing the ability to attach such devices or adding restrictions to their use. This can include limiting the connections to an allowlist of pre-screened devices based on serial number, enforcing read only access, mandatory encryption, and other restrictions. For controllers and embedded devices, where software-based device control is rarely available, the emphasis falls on physical measures such as port blockers, locked enclosures, disabled or sealed maintenance and console ports, and tamper-evident seals, producing an overlap with physical controls.}
2026-05-31 15:00:53.726 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Peripheral device control provides moderate detection and prevention capability against transient cyber asset attacks. The control directly addresses a primary vector through which transient assets introduce threats: physical connection of external hardware to ICS networks. By restricting or controlling USB ports, removable media, wireless adapters, and other peripheral connections through mechanisms like device allowlisting, port blockers, and locked enclosures, the control can detect and prevent unauthorized transient devices from connecting to the environment.\n\nHowever, the coverage is not complete for several reasons: (1) The control is primarily preventive rather than detective—it prevents connection but may not generate alerts in all implementations; (2) Transient assets authorized by legitimate personnel (contractors, vendors, maintenance personnel) may have pre-approved access, creating a window for compromise before detection; (3) The control may not detect if transient devices are already compromised before arrival, as it focuses on the connection mechanism rather than the device's security posture; (4) Some legitimate transient assets may bypass peripheral restrictions through approved temporary access credentials or pre-configured allowlists; (5) In air-gapped networks or those with effective physical security, transient asset attacks via peripheral connections may be less common than in networks with more permissive policies.\n\nThe control covers 50-75% of typical transient cyber asset attack vectors, particularly those relying on physical connection of malicious or compromised devices. It is likely to detect unskilled attackers attempting unauthorized device connections and will deter most opportunistic attacks, but determined adversaries working with insider knowledge of approved transient asset procedures may circumvent detection."}
