2026-05-31 18:21:06.794 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"SIEM systems can detect alarm suppression techniques through multiple mechanisms that align with 50-75% of attack vectors in a typical OT environment. A well-configured SIEM can: (1) correlate the absence of expected alarm messages with system state changes that should trigger alarms, detecting when alarms fail to fire when they should; (2) identify suspicious modifications to alarm configuration parameters or thresholds; (3) detect anomalous changes in alarm log patterns or sudden drops in alarm frequency; (4) correlate multiple data sources (protocol logs, I/O monitoring, system logs) to identify when alarms are being suppressed across different alarm types. However, SIEM effectiveness is limited by several factors: (1) detection relies on having baseline alarm profiles and expected alarm patterns defined, which requires significant expertise and tuning; (2) memory-resident code tampering or assembly-level instruction modifications may not generate detectable log events that reach the SIEM; (3) device display tampering may go undetected if the actual alarm state isn't logged; (4) false negatives increase in environments without comprehensive logging from all alarm sources; (5) a moderately skilled attacker who suppresses alarms while simultaneously suppressing or spoofing corresponding logs could evade detection. The control is architecturally strong for detecting many alarm suppression vectors through behavioral anomaly analysis, making it likely to catch moderately skilled attackers and automated exploitation, but sophisticated attackers with detailed knowledge of the specific logging infrastructure could circumvent detection."}
