2026-05-31 18:21:11.748 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"SIEM can detect command message blocking through network traffic analysis and correlation rules that identify abnormal patterns, such as missing expected command responses, sudden drops in command message frequencies, or unexpected latency in control system communications. SIEM systems can aggregate data from network sensors, firewalls, ICS protocol analyzers, and endpoint logs to correlate evidence of blocked messages—for example, observing a command being transmitted but never acknowledged, or detecting firewall rule changes followed by communication failures. However, the detection effectiveness depends significantly on: (1) proper instrumentation and data sources being fed into the SIEM (not all OT environments have comprehensive network visibility); (2) the sophistication of detection rules configured (many organizations have basic SIEM deployments with limited ICS-specific analytics); (3) the ability to distinguish between legitimate network issues, timeouts, and intentional message blocking; and (4) whether the blocking mechanism itself leaves detectable artifacts. A sophisticated attacker using stealthy command suppression techniques at the network layer or physically intercepting communications may evade SIEM detection if the environment lacks granular protocol-level monitoring. For moderately skilled attackers or common blocking scenarios (ACL changes, firewall rule additions, spoofed responses), SIEM can achieve reasonable detection when properly configured, but coverage varies widely across typical OT deployments. This places the rating at 3—addressing 50-75% of available vectors with moderate reliability."}
