2026-05-31 18:22:26.213 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"SIEM systems can detect command-line interface usage through multiple data sources and correlation mechanisms, making them architecturally relevant to this technique. SIEM can aggregate logs from various sources including: (1) system event logs recording CLI access and command execution, (2) authentication logs capturing interactive sessions via SSH/Telnet/RDP, (3) process execution logs showing CLI invocations, and (4) network logs detecting remote CLI access protocols. SIEM's strength lies in correlating these disparate events to identify suspicious patterns—such as unusual CLI usage times, commands run by unexpected users, or sequences of reconnaissance/execution commands that suggest adversarial activity.\n\nHowever, SIEM detection effectiveness is limited by several factors: (1) reliance on proper logging configuration and enablement—many OT devices have minimal or disabled command logging by default, (2) detection depends on quality of correlation rules—generic or poorly tuned rules will have high false negatives, (3) CLI commands themselves are often legitimate administrative activity, making baseline behavior difficult to establish, (4) some controllers may lack proper log forwarding capabilities to SIEM, and (5) attackers using legitimate credentials and normal command sequences are difficult to distinguish from authorized users.\n\nSIEM would reliably detect obvious indicators like exploitation-related commands, malware installation attempts, or access from unexpected sources, but would struggle with low-and-slow approaches using legitimate tools and normal command syntax. This covers approximately 50-75% of typical attack vectors in a well-configured OT environment with comprehensive logging, making it suitable for detecting moderately skilled attackers and most automated exploitation while missing sophisticated, targeted approaches with proper operational security."}
