2026-06-14 11:48:24.476 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Session locking is architecturally relevant to the Valid Accounts technique but offers only minimal and unreliable detection capabilities. While session locking can prevent unauthorized use of an already-authenticated session during operator absence, it does not address the core mechanics of the Valid Accounts technique, which centers on credential theft and initial authentication using compromised or default credentials.\n\nSpecific limitations:\n\n1. **No credential compromise detection**: Session locking does not detect when an attacker obtains valid credentials through phishing, credential stuffing, password spraying, or other credential access techniques. It only locks sessions after authentication has already occurred.\n\n2. **No initial access prevention**: Once an attacker authenticates using stolen credentials, session locking provides no mechanism to detect this unauthorized login. The attacker can establish a new session with legitimate credentials that will not be locked unless they remain inactive.\n\n3. **Limited to physical absence scenarios**: Session locking primarily protects against opportunistic physical access during operator absence. A remote attacker using valid credentials would not be blocked by session locking if they authenticate during an active session period or from a different terminal.\n\n4. **No behavioral analysis**: The control does not monitor for anomalous login patterns, unusual access times, or suspicious credential usage that would indicate compromised accounts.\n\n5. **No credential inventory or default password detection**: Session locking does not identify or prevent the use of default credentials, which is explicitly mentioned in the technique description.\n\nWhile session locking provides some defense-in-depth value by making it harder for an attacker to maintain presence undetected at an unattended workstation, it would only reliably de