2026-06-14 12:37:02.093 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0857, technique_name=System Firmware, technique_description=System firmware on modern assets is often designed with an update feature. Older device firmware may be factory installed and require special reprograming equipment. When available, the firmware update feature enables vendors to remotely patch bugs and perform upgrades. Device firmware updates are often delegated to the user and may be done using a software update package. It may also be possible to perform this task over the network. An adversary may exploit the firmware update feature on accessible devices to upload malicious or out-of-date firmware. Malicious modification of device firmware may provide an adversary with root access to a device, given firmware is one of the lowest programming abstraction layers., control_name=Secure boot, control_description=Secure Boot is a foundational security mechanism embedded in a device's hardware and firmware that ensures only cryptographically signed, trusted software is allowed to execute during the startup process. By systematically verifying the digital signatures of the bootloader, operating system kernel, and underlying firmware against a hardware root of trust, Secure Boot prevents attackers from loading unauthorised code, rootkits, or bootkits onto critical industrial assets like Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs), Remote Terminal Units (RTUs), and edge gateways. This guarantees that the device boots into a known-good state before any industrial logic begins, effectively neutralising deeply persistent, low-level malware that could otherwise silently manipulate physical processes while evading standard operating system defences.}
2026-06-14 12:37:07.598 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Secure Boot provides strong detection and prevention capabilities against the System Firmware technique by establishing a hardware-based root of trust that cryptographically validates firmware integrity at startup. When an adversary attempts to upload malicious firmware, Secure Boot will detect and reject any unsigned or improperly signed firmware during the boot process, preventing execution of the malicious code. This addresses the core attack vector of firmware modification by making unauthorized firmware changes detectable and non-executable.\n\nThe control achieves a rating of 4 rather than 5 because several conditions limit complete coverage: (1) Secure Boot primarily detects attacks at boot time; firmware modifications might occur but go undetected if the device never reboots after compromise, (2) some legacy ICS devices may not have Secure Boot capabilities or may have it disabled for operational compatibility reasons, (3) an attacker with sufficient access might disable Secure Boot itself before uploading malicious firmware, though this would typically trigger alerts, (4) vulnerabilities in the Secure Boot implementation itself (though rare) could potentially be exploited to bypass validation, and (5) devices with custom or vendor-controlled signing keys might be compromised if those keys are not properly protected. Additionally, Secure Boot is most effective against the remote firmware update vector but provides less detection capability if an attacker has physical access to reprogram firmware through specialized equipment outside normal update mechanisms.\n\nIn a typical OT environment with properly configured Secure Boot across 75-90% of firmware update attempts, skilled attackers would need to either disable the control (creating detectable events) or exploit implementation weaknesses, making this a strong defensive measure."}
