2026-06-14 12:37:30.055 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Secure Boot provides strong detection and prevention capabilities against rootkit deployment, particularly for firmware and bootkit-level rootkits that execute during the system startup process. The control's cryptographic verification of bootloader, kernel, and firmware signatures directly addresses the technique's core mechanism of achieving persistence at low system levels.\n\nStrengths: (1) Secure Boot effectively prevents unsigned rootkits from loading during the boot sequence by enforcing signature verification at the hardware/firmware level, making it extremely difficult for attackers to install kernel-level or firmware rootkits without detection; (2) The control specifically addresses the persistent nature of rootkits by ensuring only trusted code executes from the earliest stages of system initialization; (3) For ICS environments with hardware-enforced Secure Boot (TPM/trusted execution), detection of tampering attempts is highly reliable; (4) The control mitigates both user-mode and kernel-mode rootkit installation vectors.\n\nLimitations: (1) Secure Boot primarily detects/prevents rootkits at boot time; rootkits already resident in memory or loaded through legitimate processes after boot may not be detected; (2) If an attacker compromises the boot process before Secure Boot is enabled or gains access to firmware signing keys, the control becomes ineffective; (3) Secure Boot does not detect or monitor rootkit behavior post-initialization or runtime API interception activities; (4) Zero-day unsigned rootkits or those using stolen/compromised signing certificates may bypass detection; (5) In some ICS environments with legacy systems or devices without Secure Boot support, coverage is absent; (6) The control relies on proper implementation and administrator maintenance of the trusted root of trust.\n\nThe rating of 4 reflects that Secure Boot reliably detects and prevents th