2026-06-14 15:55:54.269 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Denial-of-service protection has limited direct alignment with detecting blocked command messages. While DoS protection can theoretically detect some anomalous traffic patterns or volumetric attacks that might incidentally block command messages, it is not specifically designed to monitor command message delivery or detect message blocking attacks. DoS protections typically focus on preventing network flooding and resource exhaustion, not on identifying when legitimate command messages are being selectively blocked or intercepted. The control may detect obvious volumetric attacks that saturate network links and incidentally block commands, but it would likely miss more sophisticated blocking techniques such as: (1) selective packet filtering/dropping by compromised intermediary devices, (2) crafted protocol violations that cause legitimate command message rejection, (3) targeted blocking through access control list manipulation, or (4) application-layer blocking that doesn't manifest as typical DoS traffic. The control provides some defense-in-depth value and might catch an unskilled attacker using crude flooding techniques, but does not reliably detect the command message blocking technique itself. A skilled attacker could block command messages through various means that would not trigger typical DoS detection thresholds."}
