2026-06-14 17:35:06.347 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0820, technique_name=Exploitation for Evasion, technique_description=Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to take advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to evade detection. Vulnerabilities may exist in software that can be used to disable or circumvent security features. Adversaries may have prior knowledge through Remote System Information Discovery about security features implemented on control devices. These device security features will likely be targeted directly for exploitation. There are examples of firmware RAM/ROM consistency checks on control devices being targeted by adversaries to enable the installation of malicious System Firmware., control_name=Runtime exploit protection, control_description=Runtime exploit protection refers to the use of builtin mechanisms such as data execution prevention (DEP), address space layout randomisation (ASLR), or others, to prevent software exploits from succeeding during normal operation where they exist. Unlike in IT environments, where such mechanisms are enabled by default, this may not be the case on all OT devices. Consult manufacturer recommendations for more guidance.}
2026-06-14 17:35:12.042 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Runtime exploit protection (DEP, ASLR, etc.) provides meaningful but incomplete detection coverage for Exploitation for Evasion in ICS environments. The control directly addresses a significant subset of the technique by preventing successful exploitation of memory-based vulnerabilities that adversaries might use to bypass security features. When enabled, these mechanisms can prevent buffer overflow, return-oriented programming (ROP), and other memory-corruption attacks that could disable detection mechanisms or firmware integrity checks.\n\nHowever, the coverage has notable limitations:\n\n1. **Scope Limitations**: The control primarily addresses memory-based exploitation vectors. Many OT devices use firmware-based or hardware-based security mechanisms (e.g., secure boot, firmware verification, integrity checks) that may not be adequately protected by runtime exploit protections alone.\n\n2. **ICS-Specific Gaps**: The technique specifically mentions targeting firmware RAM/ROM consistency checks. Runtime exploit protection on the main OS may not extend to firmware-level vulnerabilities, bootloader exploits, or JTAG/debug port access—common vectors in OT environments.\n\n3. **Inconsistent Enablement**: As the control description notes, these mechanisms are not enabled by default on many OT devices. Coverage effectiveness is highly dependent on proper configuration and varies significantly across device types and manufacturers, limiting the reliability of detection in a typical OT environment.\n\n4. **Detection vs. Prevention**: While the control prevents successful exploitation, it does not detect the exploitation attempt itself. An attacker might trigger protection mechanisms that appear as benign anomalies rather than clear attack indicators.\n\n5. **Non-Exploitative Evasion**: Some evasion techniques may not rely on software exploitation (e.g., physical attacks, firmware modification through legitimate debug interfaces, supply chain compromises).\n\nThe control would reliably detect 50-75% of exploitation-based evasion attempts in well-configured environments, particularly those targeting user-mode vulnerabilities, but falls short on firmware-level, bootloader-level, and non-standard exploitation vectors common in OT settings."}
2026-06-14 17:35:16.027 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0890, technique_name=Exploitation for Privilege Escalation, technique_description=Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in an attempt to elevate privileges. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Security constructs such as permission levels will often hinder access to information and use of certain techniques, so adversaries will likely need to perform privilege escalation to include use of software exploitation to circumvent those restrictions. When initially gaining access to a system, an adversary may be operating within a lower privileged process which will prevent them from accessing certain resources on the system. Vulnerabilities may exist, usually in operating system components and software commonly running at higher permissions, that can be exploited to gain higher levels of access on the system., control_name=Runtime exploit protection, control_description=Runtime exploit protection refers to the use of builtin mechanisms such as data execution prevention (DEP), address space layout randomisation (ASLR), or others, to prevent software exploits from succeeding during normal operation where they exist. Unlike in IT environments, where such mechanisms are enabled by default, this may not be the case on all OT devices. Consult manufacturer recommendations for more guidance.}
2026-06-14 17:35:23.905 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Runtime exploit protection mechanisms (DEP, ASLR, stack canaries, etc.) provide moderate detection and prevention capability against privilege escalation exploits in OT environments. The control directly addresses the technical mechanism of T0890 by preventing many common exploitation vectors from succeeding, which would inherently detect attempted exploits through their failure or abnormal behavior patterns.\n\nStrengths: (1) DEP prevents execution of injected shellcode in data segments, blocking a primary exploit delivery method; (2) ASLR defeats many traditional ROP-based and return-to-libc exploits by randomizing memory layouts; (3) Stack canaries detect stack buffer overflow attempts; (4) These mechanisms provide runtime detection/prevention without requiring signature knowledge of specific vulnerabilities. If exploits fail due to these protections, detection occurs through exception handling and crash reporting.\n\nLimitations preventing higher coverage: (1) Exploitation techniques using non-memory corruption vectors (e.g., logic flaws, race conditions, legitimate API abuse) may bypass these protections entirely; (2) Not all OT systems have these mechanisms enabled by default, as the control description notes, limiting practical coverage in heterogeneous ICS environments; (3) Skilled attackers can develop exploit mitigations specific to the target environment (e.g., leaking ASLR offsets, brute-forcing addresses on systems with limited address space); (4) Detection is reactive rather than proactive—the control confirms that an exploit attempt occurred only after it fails; (5) Some modern exploitation techniques (e.g., control-flow hijacking using legitimate code gadgets) can succeed even with these protections; (6) Zero-day exploits or unpredictable vulnerability patterns may succeed before patches are available.\n\nThe control achieves approximately 50-75% coverage of common 