2026-06-14 17:35:28.852 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0866, technique_name=Exploitation of Remote Services, technique_description=Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to take advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to enable remote service abuse. A common goal for post-compromise exploitation of remote services is for initial access into and lateral movement throughout the ICS environment to enable access to targeted systems. ICS asset owners and operators have been affected by ransomware (or disruptive malware masquerading as ransomware) migrating from enterprise IT to ICS environments: WannaCry, NotPetya, and BadRabbit. In each of these cases, self-propagating (wormable) malware initially infected IT networks, but through exploit spread to industrial networks, producing significant impacts., control_name=Runtime exploit protection, control_description=Runtime exploit protection refers to the use of builtin mechanisms such as data execution prevention (DEP), address space layout randomisation (ASLR), or others, to prevent software exploits from succeeding during normal operation where they exist. Unlike in IT environments, where such mechanisms are enabled by default, this may not be the case on all OT devices. Consult manufacturer recommendations for more guidance.}
2026-06-14 17:35:34.244 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Runtime exploit protection mechanisms (DEP, ASLR, etc.) provide defense-in-depth against exploitation techniques but have significant limitations in detecting T0866 in ICS environments. These controls primarily prevent exploit execution rather than detect exploitation attempts. While they can prevent some exploits from succeeding, they do not generate alerts or visibility when exploitation is attempted—they simply cause the attack to fail silently or crash the application. This means detection is indirect at best.\n\nKey limitations:\n1. No direct detection capability: These mechanisms prevent exploitation but do not inherently log or alert on exploitation attempts, so security teams may not know an attack occurred.\n2. Inconsistent deployment in OT: Unlike IT environments where DEP/ASLR are typically enabled by default, OT devices often run with these protections disabled for performance reasons or manufacturer compatibility, significantly reducing coverage across typical ICS environments.\n3. Advanced exploits may bypass: Sophisticated attackers familiar with exploit techniques can develop exploits that work around ASLR/DEP through information leakage, ROP chains, or other advanced methods.\n4. Limited to memory-based exploits: These protections primarily address memory corruption exploits; other vulnerability classes (logic bugs, authentication bypasses, etc.) may not be mitigated.\n5. Partial coverage: While these controls may prevent some wormable malware variants (like WannaCry through EternalBlue exploitation), they represent only one layer of a multi-vector exploitation landscape.\n\nThe control would likely detect unsophisticated attackers or prevent automated worm propagation in well-configured environments, justifying a rating of 2 rather than lower."}
2026-06-14 17:35:38.326 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0814, technique_name=Denial of Service, technique_description=Adversaries may perform Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks to disrupt expected device functionality. Examples of DoS attacks include overwhelming the target device with a high volume of requests in a short time period and sending the target device a request it does not know how to handle. Disrupting device state may temporarily render it unresponsive, possibly lasting until a reboot can occur. Adversaries may also attempt to execute a Permanent Denial-of-Service (PDoS) against certain devices. Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to cause a denial of service by taking advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Vulnerabilities may exist in software that can be used to cause a denial of service condition., control_name=Runtime exploit protection, control_description=Runtime exploit protection refers to the use of builtin mechanisms such as data execution prevention (DEP), address space layout randomisation (ASLR), or others, to prevent software exploits from succeeding during normal operation where they exist. Unlike in IT environments, where such mechanisms are enabled by default, this may not be the case on all OT devices. Consult manufacturer recommendations for more guidance.}
