2026-06-14 17:45:27.908 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Static network configuration provides moderate detection and prevention capability against adversary-in-the-middle attacks. The control directly addresses several common AiTM vectors: by eliminating DHCP, it prevents attackers from obtaining network access through dynamic protocol exploitation; by binding MAC addresses to specific switch ports, it makes ARP poisoning significantly more difficult since spoofed ARP responses will be rejected by switch port security; and by hardcoding routing tables, it prevents route manipulation attacks. However, the control has notable limitations. It does not inherently detect AiTM attacks that successfully occur—it primarily prevents or makes them harder to execute. A sufficiently privileged attacker who gains access to network infrastructure (e.g., a compromised switch or router) could still perform AiTM attacks through legitimate means such as port mirroring or VLAN manipulation, which static configuration alone would not detect. Additionally, proxy-based AiTM attacks at the application layer may not be directly prevented by network-layer static configuration. The control is more preventative than detective in nature. In a typical OT environment with best-in-class implementation, static configuration would effectively block 50-75% of common AiTM attack vectors (particularly unsophisticated ARP poisoning and rogue DHCP attacks), making it suitable for detecting moderately skilled attackers employing standard techniques, but not advanced attackers with deep network infrastructure access."}
