2026-06-14 18:37:09.753 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"User security training is architecturally relevant to T0831 (Manipulation of Control) in that it can help operators recognize anomalous physical process behaviors or unexpected latency that might indicate control manipulation is occurring. The control's emphasis on ICS-specific training and awareness of operational safety risks provides a theoretical foundation for detecting when control systems have been compromised.\n\nHowever, the control offers no reliable detection capability for the actual technique itself. User awareness and training are fundamentally reactive and dependent on human vigilance rather than technical detection mechanisms. The control does not address:\n\n1. Detection of actual setpoint changes or parameter modifications—whether through direct device manipulation, protocol-level commands, or legitimate engineering interfaces compromised by attackers\n2. Network-based or system-level detection of unauthorized control commands\n3. Technical monitoring of control system outputs or actuator behavior\n4. Validation of command authenticity or authorization\n5. Automated alerts for anomalous control values or sequences\n\nWhile trained operators might eventually notice that a physical process is behaving abnormally (e.g., pressure rising unexpectedly, temperature deviating from setpoint), this detection depends entirely on operator attention, varies widely based on training quality, and represents a significant delay between manipulation and detection. Skilled attackers may make gradual changes, introduce plausible-appearing setpoint adjustments during maintenance windows, or manipulate control during periods when operators are less likely to notice. The control provides general defense-in-depth value through security awareness but does not constitute a reliable technical detection mechanism for the manipulation technique itself."}
