2026-06-14 18:47:57.028 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Input validation provides moderate detection capability against certain DoS attack vectors in ICS environments, particularly those exploiting malformed packets or out-of-bounds parameters. The control directly addresses DoS attacks that rely on sending malformed or unexpected data that would normally crash or disrupt device functionality—such as oversized temperature setpoints, invalid command structures, or protocol violations. Deep Packet Inspection firewalls and parameter bounds-checking can effectively detect and reject these attack vectors before they reach critical devices.\n\nHowever, input validation has significant limitations against other common DoS techniques. It cannot effectively detect or mitigate volumetric attacks (e.g., flooding with legitimate-looking packets), slowloris-style attacks that exhaust resources through slowness rather than malformation, or attacks exploiting valid but resource-intensive commands. Additionally, input validation may not detect sophisticated attacks that craft data within acceptable parameters but trigger unintended device behavior, or attacks targeting protocol-level state management rather than packet content.\n\nThe control is sufficient to detect roughly 50-75% of DoS attack vectors, particularly those involving malformed inputs or parameter violations that would affect 25-50% of typical OT environments. It would likely detect unsophisticated attackers who rely on obviously malformed packets, but a moderately skilled attacker could bypass this control using legitimate packet formats or volumetric methods. The rating of 3 reflects reliable detection of input-validation-exploiting DoS vectors while acknowledging substantial gaps against other DoS methodologies."}
2026-06-14 18:48:01.535 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0830, technique_name=Adversary-in-the-Middle, technique_description=Adversaries with privileged network access may seek to modify network traffic in real time using adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) attacks. This type of attack allows the adversary to intercept traffic to and/or from a particular device on the network. If a AiTM attack is established, then the adversary has the ability to block, log, modify, or inject traffic into the communication stream. There are several ways to accomplish this attack, but some of the most-common are Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) poisoning and the use of a proxy., control_name=Input validation, control_description=Input validation is a critical software and protocol-level security control that rigorously inspects and sanitises incoming data before it is processed by a system, ensuring that commands and variables fall strictly within safe, expected engineering parameters. Historically, many legacy industrial protocols like Modbus or DNP3 were designed with implicit trust, meaning a Programmable Logic Controller (PLC) might blindly accept a maliciously crafted packet or a drastically out-of-bounds setpoint (such as a temperature command of 9999 degrees instead of 90), potentially leading to memory corruption, controller crashes, or severe physical damage. By implementing robust input validation, often through Deep Packet Inspection (DPI) firewalls at the network edge, strict parameter constraints within Human-Machine Interface (HMI) input fields, and hard-coded bounds-checking within the PLC logic itself, applications can systematically detect and reject malformed traffic and dangerous commands.}
2026-06-14 18:48:05.930 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Input validation is architecturally relevant to detecting some consequences of adversary-in-the-middle attacks but offers unreliable detection of the AiTM technique itself. While input validation can reject maliciously modified commands or out-of-bounds values that might result from an AiTM attack (e.g., detecting an injected command with invalid parameters), it does not detect the actual interception, modification, or injection mechanisms that characterize AiTM attacks. An attacker executing an AiTM attack via ARP poisoning or proxy can craft well-formed, parameter-compliant packets that pass input validation filters while still achieving the attacker's objectives. Input validation operates at the application layer and validates semantic correctness of data, whereas AiTM attacks operate at the network layer and succeed regardless of whether injected traffic conforms to expected input ranges. Detection of AiTM requires network-layer controls such as ARP spoofing detection, DHCP snooping, port security, or cryptographic authentication (e.g., TLS, digital signatures) that verify traffic authenticity and integrity—none of which are provided by input validation alone. In edge cases where an attacker makes mistakes (e.g., injecting deliberately malformed packets), input validation might incidentally detect the attack, but this is not reliable detection of the technique's core mechanism."}
2026-06-14 18:48:09.799 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0831, technique_name=Manipulation of Control, technique_description=Adversaries may manipulate physical process control within the industrial environment. Methods of manipulating control can include changes to set point values, tags, or other parameters. Adversaries may manipulate control systems devices or possibly leverage their own, to communicate with and command physical control processes. The duration of manipulation may be temporary or longer sustained, depending on operator detection. Methods of Manipulation of Control include: Man-in-the-middle, Spoof command message, Changing setpoints., control_name=Input validation, control_description=Input validation is a critical software and protocol-level security control that rigorously inspects and sanitises incoming data before it is processed by a system, ensuring that commands and variables fall strictly within safe, expected engineering parameters. Historically, many legacy industrial protocols like Modbus or DNP3 were designed with implicit trust, meaning a Programmable Logic Controller (PLC) might blindly accept a maliciously crafted packet or a drastically out-of-bounds setpoint (such as a temperature command of 9999 degrees instead of 90), potentially leading to memory corruption, controller crashes, or severe physical damage. By implementing robust input validation, often through Deep Packet Inspection (DPI) firewalls at the network edge, strict parameter constraints within Human-Machine Interface (HMI) input fields, and hard-coded bounds-checking within the PLC logic itself, applications can systematically detect and reject malformed traffic and dangerous commands.}
2026-06-14 18:48:14.231 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Input validation provides strong detection capabilities for T0831 (Manipulation of Control) across multiple attack vectors. The control directly addresses the core mechanisms of control manipulation by validating that incoming commands and setpoint values conform to expected engineering parameters and safe operating ranges. This is particularly effective against the primary attack vectors described in the technique: spoof command messages (detected when message format/structure violates validation rules), changing setpoints (detected when out-of-bounds values are rejected), and man-in-the-middle attacks that inject malicious commands (detected through validation of command syntax and parameter constraints). The control's effectiveness across Deep Packet Inspection firewalls, HMI input validation, and PLC-level bounds-checking provides defense-in-depth coverage that would catch most standard manipulation attempts. However, the rating does not reach 5 because: (1) a highly skilled attacker might craft commands that fall within valid parameter ranges but still achieve harmful physical outcomes through subtle manipulation of legitimate setpoints; (2) input validation may not detect all protocol-level spoofing techniques if the validation is incomplete or improperly configured; (3) some legacy systems may have overly permissive parameter ranges that allow dangerous but 'valid' commands; and (4) input validation alone cannot prevent manipulation if an attacker has legitimate credentials or compromised operator access. The control is likely to detect unskilled and moderately skilled attackers attempting straightforward command injection or grossly out-of-bounds setpoint manipulation."}
2026-06-14 18:58:00.905 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0814, technique_name=Denial of Service, technique_description=Adversaries may perform Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks to disrupt expected device functionality. Examples of DoS attacks include overwhelming the target device with a high volume of requests in a short time period and sending the target device a request it does not know how to handle. Disrupting device state may temporarily render it unresponsive, possibly lasting until a reboot can occur. When placed in this state, devices may be unable to send and receive requests, and may not perform expected response functions in reaction to other events in the environment. Some ICS devices are particularly sensitive to DoS events, and may become unresponsive in reaction to even a simple ping sweep. Adversaries may also attempt to execute a Permanent Denial-of-Service (PDoS) against certain devices, such as in the case of the BrickerBot malware. Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to cause a denial of service by taking advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Vulnerabilities may exist in software that can be used to cause a denial of service condition. Adversaries may have prior knowledge about industrial protocols or control devices used in the environment through Remote System Information Discovery. There are examples of adversaries remotely causing a Device Restart/Shutdown by exploiting a vulnerability that induces uncontrolled resource consumption., control_name=Watchdog timers, control_description=Watchdog timers act as an autonomous, low-level health monitoring mechanism designed to detect and recover from severe system faults or lockups within critical controllers like Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs) and Remote Terminal Units (RTUs). Implemented as either a dedicated hardware circuit or a software routine, a watchdog requires the primary control program to periodically send a signal to reset a continuous countdown. If a condition, including a cyber attack, causes the controller's logic to freeze, crash, or enter an infinite loop, the expected reset signal will fail to arrive. Upon the timer expiring, the watchdog automatically overrides the frozen system to trigger a predefined failsafe action, such as executing a hard reboot, alerting a supervisory system, or forcing physical outputs into a safe, de-energised state. Watchdogs are a common feature of safety systems and therefore rarely impose additional costs as security controls. However, it should be noted that nuisance tripping of watchdogs can cause false positive alarms or operational disruption, if the predefined response to a watchdog trip forces a system offline.}
2026-06-14 18:58:05.619 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Watchdog timers provide limited detection coverage for DoS attacks (T0814) in ICS environments. They can detect certain categories of DoS that cause system lockups, crashes, or infinite loops (such as resource exhaustion leading to frozen logic), as the watchdog will trigger when the expected reset signal fails to arrive. However, watchdog timers have significant gaps in coverage: (1) They do not detect DoS attacks that cause temporary disruption without system lockup (e.g., high-volume network flooding that reduces responsiveness but doesn't freeze the processor), (2) They cannot detect PDoS (Permanent Denial-of-Service) attacks that cause hardware damage, (3) They do not detect DoS attacks exploiting protocol vulnerabilities that don't result in system freezing, (4) They provide no visibility into attack mechanisms—they only respond after the fact when damage is already done, (5) Watchdog activation indicates a problem occurred but provides minimal forensic or real-time detection capability to identify the attack as it happens. The control is architecturally relevant to one subset of DoS vectors (those causing computation stalls) and would detect an unskilled attacker using simple resource exhaustion, but it fails to address the majority of DoS attack vectors in a typical OT environment. Additionally, the control risks operational disruption through false positives or nuisance trips, limiting its practical utility. This assessment assumes best-in-class watchdog configuration and placement across critical controllers."}
