2026-06-14 18:58:24.159 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Watchdog timers have limited direct detection capability for the T0816 Device Restart/Shutdown technique. While watchdog timers can detect certain fault conditions that might occur as a consequence of a restart/shutdown attack (such as a device failing to respond with the expected periodic signal), they are not designed to specifically detect or distinguish intentional restart/shutdown commands from legitimate operational events. The control may detect that a restart/shutdown has occurred through the absence of watchdog reset signals, but this is an indirect indicator rather than a direct detection mechanism. Additionally, a watchdog timer would only detect the restart/shutdown if it occurs while the watchdog monitoring is active; an attacker could issue a restart command that completes the reboot cycle before the watchdog mechanism can react. The control also cannot differentiate between authorized maintenance restarts and malicious shutdown commands. Furthermore, if an attacker simply restarts a device and the device boots cleanly with the watchdog properly configured, the watchdog will not raise an alert—it will simply resume normal monitoring. For a skilled attacker, this control provides minimal impedance to executing device restart/shutdown attacks. The control is more aligned with detecting fault conditions and system lockups rather than identifying intentional device restart/shutdown commands issued through legitimate interfaces (web UI, CLI, protocols). This rating reflects that watchdog timers may detect some instances where restart/shutdown causes system anomalies, but they cannot reliably detect the technique itself across typical OT environments."}
