2026-06-14 18:59:10.313 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Watchdog timers provide limited detection capability for rootkits in ICS environments. The control can indirectly detect some rootkit impacts by identifying system behavior anomalies—specifically, if a rootkit causes the primary control program to freeze, crash, or enter an infinite loop (preventing the watchdog reset signal), the timer will expire and trigger a failsafe action. This represents detection of the rootkit's behavioral consequences rather than detection of the rootkit itself.\n\nHowever, the control has significant limitations against rootkit techniques:\n\n1. **Stealthy rootkits bypass detection**: Many rootkits are designed to hide their presence while maintaining normal system operation. If a rootkit successfully intercepts and modifies OS API calls without disrupting the control program's ability to send periodic reset signals, the watchdog will not detect it.\n\n2. **Firmware rootkits are largely undetectable**: The technique explicitly mentions firmware rootkits affecting the main processing board or I/O modules. Watchdog timers cannot detect firmware-level modifications that maintain system responsiveness.\n\n3. **API call interception goes unnoticed**: The core mechanism of rootkits—intercepting and modifying operating-system API calls—occurs at levels below what watchdog timers typically monitor. A rootkit can hide malware, false files, or unauthorized network connections while the system continues to function normally.\n\n4. **Detection only occurs with operational impact**: The watchdog only triggers when the rootkit actually causes system dysfunction (freezing, crashing, infinite loops). Rootkits designed to operate silently while maintaining system stability will not be detected.\n\n5. **False negatives from covert persistence**: A rootkit providing persistent backdoor access without causing system disruption will evade the watchdog entirely.\n\nThe control addresses approximately 25-50% of rootkit scenarios—primarily those where the rootkit's malicious activity directly interferes with normal system operation—making it suitable for detecting unskilled attackers or obvious implementation errors, but insufficient against moderately sophisticated rootkit deployments."}
