2026-06-14 19:17:13.012 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0801, technique_name=Monitor Process State, technique_description=Adversaries may gather information about the physical process state. This information may be used to gain more information about the process itself or used as a trigger for malicious actions. The sources of process state information may vary such as, OPC tags, historian data, specific PLC block information, or network traffic., control_name=Configuration audit, control_description=A configuration audit is the systematic review and validation of the software, firmware, network, and logic settings across operational technology (OT) assets—such as Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs), industrial firewalls, and Human-Machine Interfaces (HMIs) to ensure they strictly align with a documented, "known-good" baseline. This process is essential for detecting "configuration drift," which can occur over time due to undocumented maintenance tweaks, accidental engineering errors, or malicious tampering.}
2026-06-14 19:17:17.135 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Configuration audit is architecturally relevant to T0801 (Monitor Process State) but offers no reliable detection of the technique itself. While configuration audits can verify that monitoring systems, OPC servers, and data collection tools are properly configured according to baseline standards, they are fundamentally passive assessments of static settings rather than active detection mechanisms. Configuration audits would NOT detect: (1) an attacker querying existing OPC tags to gather process state information, (2) an attacker reading historian data through legitimate channels, (3) an attacker analyzing network traffic to infer process state, or (4) an attacker extracting information from PLC memory or registers during runtime. The technique describes information gathering—an adversary observing or reading process state data—which occurs dynamically during an attack, whereas configuration audits only validate that configurations match a baseline. An audit might detect if unauthorized data sources or monitoring tools were added to a system (configuration drift), but this represents only an indirect and unreliable indicator. A skilled attacker gathering process state information through legitimate, expected queries would generate no configuration changes and thus remain undetected. Configuration audits provide defense-in-depth value by ensuring monitoring tools are properly deployed, but they do not functionally detect the act of monitoring or accessing process state data itself."}
