2026-06-14 19:18:01.039 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Configuration audit is architecturally relevant to T0893 (Data from Local System) but offers no reliable detection of the technique itself. While configuration audits validate that system settings align with known-good baselines, they are fundamentally preventive and baseline-comparison controls—not detective controls designed to identify active data exfiltration or unauthorized file access. Configuration audits focus on detecting unauthorized *changes* to configurations, firmware, and logic settings, not on detecting the act of adversaries reading, querying, or collecting data from local file systems, databases, or configuration files. An attacker performing T0893 (e.g., using command-line tools or scripts to read schematics, specifications, or configuration data) would not necessarily alter system configurations, and thus would likely evade detection by a configuration audit. The control would only detect T0893 if the attacker modified system configurations as a side-effect of their data collection activity—an unlikely and non-standard implementation scenario. To effectively detect T0893, organizations would need endpoint detection and response (EDR), file integrity monitoring, command-line auditing, or data loss prevention (DLP) controls that specifically monitor for file access, command execution, or data movement patterns."}
