2026-06-14 19:18:05.116 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Configuration audit is highly effective at detecting modifications to alarm settings because it compares current configurations against a documented baseline. When an adversary modifies alarm settings—such as disabling alarms, raising thresholds, or changing notification parameters—these changes create configuration drift that a systematic audit would identify. The control's strength lies in its comprehensive approach: it validates settings across OT assets including PLCs, HMIs, and industrial systems where alarm configurations are typically stored and managed. The audit process would detect most malicious alarm modifications since they must persist in the device's configuration to be effective. However, the coverage rating of 4 rather than 5 reflects several limiting factors: (1) the effectiveness depends on audit frequency and scope—if audits are infrequent or incomplete, modifications might persist undetected for extended periods; (2) some alarm modifications might be subtle or occur in system-generated logs that are easier to manipulate than configuration files; (3) in environments with poor documentation or unclear baselines, distinguishing legitimate changes from malicious ones becomes harder; (4) adversaries with deep system knowledge might make changes that appear consistent with authorized configuration practices, potentially evading detection if the baseline itself is compromised. The control would reliably detect the technique in most scenarios but may miss edge cases involving sophisticated attackers with knowledge of authorized configuration practices or operations."}
2026-06-14 19:27:49.229 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0862, technique_name=Supply Chain Compromise, technique_description=Adversaries may perform supply chain compromise to gain control systems environment access by means of infected products, software, and workflows. Supply chain compromise is the manipulation of products, such as devices or software, or their delivery mechanisms before receipt by the end consumer. Adversary compromise of these products and mechanisms is done for the goal of data or system compromise, once infected products are introduced to the target environment. Supply chain compromise can occur at all stages of the supply chain, from manipulation of development tools and environments to manipulation of developed products and tools distribution mechanisms. This may involve the compromise and replacement of legitimate software and patches, such as on third party or vendor websites. Targeting of supply chain compromise can be done in attempts to infiltrate the environments of a specific audience. In control systems environments with assets in both the IT and OT networks, it is possible a supply chain compromise affecting the IT environment could enable further access to the OT environment. Counterfeit devices may be introduced to the global supply chain posing safety and cyber risks to asset owners and operators. These devices may not meet the safety, engineering and manufacturing requirements of regulatory bodies but may feature tagging indicating conformance with industry standards. Due to the lack of adherence to standards and overall lesser quality, the counterfeit products may pose a serious safety and operational risk. Yokogawa identified instances in which their customers received counterfeit differential pressure transmitters using the Yokogawa logo. The counterfeit transmitters were nearly indistinguishable with a semblance of functionality and interface that mimics the genuine product. F-Secure Labs analyzed the approach the adversary used to compromise victim systems with Havex. The adversary planted trojanized software installers available on legitimate ICS/SCADA vendor websites. After being downloaded, this software infected the host computer with a Remote Access Trojan (RAT)., control_name=Supply chain security management, control_description=Supply chain security management describes processes aimed at mitigating the complex risks introduced by third-party vendors, system integrators, and the external procurement of hardware and software to prevent supply chain compromise. Such processes include vendor risk assessments, Software Bill of Materials (SBOMs) requirements to identify hidden vulnerabilities in embedded code, and security standards that vendors and contractors must adhere to.}
2026-06-14 19:27:53.860 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Supply chain security management addresses supply chain compromise through preventive and detective mechanisms that cover a moderate to significant portion of attack vectors. The control's effectiveness includes: (1) Vendor risk assessments that can identify vendors with poor security practices or those already compromised, reducing the likelihood of accepting malicious products; (2) SBOM requirements that enable identification of hidden vulnerabilities and potentially detect known compromised components or suspicious dependencies in software; (3) Security standards enforcement that establishes baseline requirements vendors must meet, filtering out counterfeit or non-compliant devices. However, the control has notable limitations: (1) It primarily operates as a preventive control rather than a detective control—it cannot directly detect when compromise has already occurred in the supply chain before products reach the organization; (2) Sophisticated adversaries may evade vendor risk assessments by compromising trusted vendors or masquerading as legitimate vendors; (3) SBOMs require accurate vendor cooperation and may not reveal zero-day vulnerabilities or advanced persistent threats embedded in products; (4) The control cannot detect supply chain compromise that occurs after initial product delivery or in services/workflows outside formal procurement channels; (5) Counterfeit device detection relies on physical inspection and authentication mechanisms that may not be consistently implemented. The control would likely detect approximately 50-75% of supply chain compromise attempts in a typical environment, particularly those involving less sophisticated adversaries or publicly available compromised products, but would struggle against determined, well-resourced attackers who can compromise established vendors or introduce advanced threats undetectable through standard procurement processes."}
2026-06-14 19:27:57.733 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0857, technique_name=System Firmware, technique_description=System firmware on modern assets is often designed with an update feature. Older device firmware may be factory installed and require special reprograming equipment. When available, the firmware update feature enables vendors to remotely patch bugs and perform upgrades. Device firmware updates are often delegated to the user and may be done using a software update package. It may also be possible to perform this task over the network. An adversary may exploit the firmware update feature on accessible devices to upload malicious or out-of-date firmware. Malicious modification of device firmware may provide an adversary with root access to a device, given firmware is one of the lowest programming abstraction layers., control_name=Supply chain security management, control_description=Supply chain security management describes processes aimed at mitigating the complex risks introduced by third-party vendors, system integrators, and the external procurement of hardware and software to prevent supply chain compromise. Such processes include vendor risk assessments, Software Bill of Materials (SBOMs) requirements to identify hidden vulnerabilities in embedded code, and security standards that vendors and contractors must adhere to.}
2026-06-14 19:28:01.486 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Supply chain security management is architecturally relevant to System Firmware attacks but offers minimal detection capability for active exploitation. The control addresses upstream prevention through vendor assessments, SBOM requirements, and security standards that could theoretically reduce the likelihood of compromised firmware reaching deployment. However, it does not provide reliable detection of firmware manipulation or unauthorized firmware updates once assets are operational in the ICS environment. The control focuses on pre-deployment vetting rather than runtime detection of firmware modifications, malicious firmware uploads, or unauthorized update activities. An attacker using legitimate update mechanisms, exploiting zero-days unknown at procurement time, or performing firmware modifications post-deployment would likely evade this control. While supply chain practices may reduce exposure to some pre-compromise scenarios, they cannot detect actual firmware exploitation attempts in the field without complementary runtime monitoring controls (such as firmware integrity verification, update authorization logging, or network-based update traffic analysis). The control would only detect firmware compromise if it were identifiable during initial procurement activities—a scenario unlikely for sophisticated, zero-day-based attacks or post-deployment modifications."}
2026-06-14 19:28:09.095 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0839, technique_name=Module Firmware, technique_description=Adversaries may install malicious or vulnerable firmware onto modular hardware devices. Control system devices often contain modular hardware devices. These devices may have their own set of firmware that is separate from the firmware of the main control system equipment. This technique is similar to System Firmware, but is conducted on other system components that may not have the same capabilities or level of integrity checking. Although it results in a device re-image, malicious device firmware may provide persistent access to remaining devices. An easy point of access for an adversary is the Ethernet card, which may have its own CPU, RAM, and operating system. The adversary may attack and likely exploit the computer on an Ethernet card. Exploitation of the Ethernet card computer may enable the adversary to accomplish additional attacks, such as the following: Delayed Attack - The adversary may stage an attack in advance and choose when to launch it, such as at a particularly damaging time. Brick the Ethernet Card - Malicious firmware may be programmed to result in an Ethernet card failure, requiring a factory return. Random Attack or Failure - The adversary may load malicious firmware onto multiple field devices. Execution of an attack and the time it occurs is generated by a pseudo-random number generator. A Field Device Worm - The adversary may choose to identify all field devices of the same model, with the end goal of performing a device-wide compromise. Attack Other Cards on the Field Device - Although it is not the most important module in a field device, the Ethernet card is most accessible to the CPU module., control_name=Supply chain security management, control_description=Supply chain security management describes processes aimed at mitigating the complex risks introduced by third-party vendors, system integrators, and the external procurement of hardware and software to prevent supply chain compromise. Such processes include vendor risk assessments, Software Bill of Materials (SBOMs) requirements to identify hidden vulnerabilities in embedded code, and security standards that vendors and contractors must adhere to.}
2026-06-14 19:28:12.764 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Supply chain security management is architecturally relevant to Module Firmware attacks but offers limited detection capability. The control focuses on preventing compromise during procurement and vendor vetting through practices like vendor risk assessments, SBOM requirements, and security standards adherence. While these measures may help prevent malicious firmware from being introduced at the manufacturing or initial distribution stage, they do not detect the actual installation or execution of malicious firmware on modular hardware devices after deployment. The control is preventive rather than detective in nature. Once a device is in the field, supply chain controls provide minimal ability to detect unauthorized firmware modifications on modular components like Ethernet cards. An attacker with physical access or remote capabilities could still load malicious firmware onto these modules, and supply chain processes would not reliably detect such post-deployment compromises. The control would only detect the technique if the adversary made a significant error (e.g., using a vendor already flagged during assessment) or if the malicious firmware was traced back to a compromised supply chain source. Detection of actual firmware modifications on deployed devices would require different controls such as firmware integrity monitoring, endpoint detection and response, or device-level attestation mechanisms—none of which are inherent to supply chain security management."}
2026-06-14 19:28:17.803 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0847, technique_name=Replication Through Removable Media, technique_description=Adversaries may move onto systems, such as those separated from the enterprise network, by copying malware to removable media which is inserted into the control systems environment. The adversary may rely on unknowing trusted third parties, such as suppliers or contractors with access privileges, to introduce the removable media. This technique enables initial access to target devices that never connect to untrusted networks, but are physically accessible. Operators of the German nuclear power plant, Gundremmingen, discovered malware on a facility computer not connected to the internet. The malware included Conficker and W32.Ramnit, which were also found on eighteen removable disk drives in the facility. The plant has since checked for infection and cleaned up more than 1,000 computers. An ESET researcher commented that internet disconnection does not guarantee system safety from infection or payload execution., control_name=Supply chain security management, control_description=Supply chain security management describes processes aimed at mitigating the complex risks introduced by third-party vendors, system integrators, and the external procurement of hardware and software to prevent supply chain compromise. Such processes include vendor risk assessments, Software Bill of Materials (SBOMs) requirements to identify hidden vulnerabilities in embedded code, and security standards that vendors and contractors must adhere to.}
2026-06-14 19:28:21.876 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Supply chain security management has limited functional alignment with detecting replication through removable media. While the control addresses vendor risk assessments and security standards that contractors must follow, it is primarily preventative rather than detective in nature. The control can reduce the likelihood of malicious actors being used as vectors (by vetting contractors and imposing security requirements), but it does not provide direct mechanisms to detect when removable media containing malware is actually introduced into the environment. The control does not address: (1) detection of unauthorized removable media insertion, (2) scanning of removable media for malicious content, (3) monitoring of file transfers from removable devices, or (4) technical controls that would identify actual malware execution from removable media. Detection would only occur if a vendor is discovered to be compromised or deliberately malicious during assessments—a scenario that requires the supply chain compromise to be identified through other means. SBOM requirements might help identify vulnerable embedded code, but this does not detect active malware replication attempts. For a moderately skilled attacker, this control would offer minimal detection capability if the attacker is introduced as a trusted third party with valid credentials and access. The control is positioned at 2 because it addresses only the prevention and vetting aspects of the supply chain vector (approximately 25-50% of detection opportunity), but lacks technical detection mechanisms for the actual malware replication event itself."}
